Confirmed via code read: `all()` uses prepared statements with bound params only for `?` placeholders, but `sort`/`column` are string-interpolated directly into the SQL text before reaching `prepare`, so no parameter binding protects the injected ORDER BY clause.

```json
{"confirmed":[
{"file":"src/routes/api.ts","line":36,"severity":"high","issue":"Unsanitized sort parameter passed into raw SQL ORDER BY clause, enabling SQL injection","evidence":"src/routes/api.ts:36 `const sort = (req.query.sort as string) || 'r.created_at DESC';` then src/routes/api.ts:37 calls `Stats.getReviewsSorted(bookId, sort)`. In src/models/stats.ts:46 the value is interpolated directly: `ORDER BY ${sort}` inside a template literal passed to `all()`, which only binds the `?` placeholders (bookId) via sql.js `prepare`/`bind` (src/db/index.ts:84-93) — the ORDER BY text itself is not parameterized. A request like `/api/books/1/reviews?sort=(SELECT 1 UNION SELECT username FROM users)--` is executed as literal SQL. No whitelist exists here, unlike the sibling `getBooksPage` which validates sortKey against `BOOK_SORT_COLUMNS` (stats.ts:31)."},
{"file":"src/routes/api.ts","line":201,"severity":"high","issue":"Rating bounds check uses && instead of ||, making the validation always pass (no actual range restriction)","evidence":"src/routes/api.ts:201: `if (!Number.isInteger(rating) || (rating < 1 && rating > 5))`. For any integer, `rating < 1 && rating > 5` cannot be true (nothing is both <1 and >5), so only the Number.isInteger check has effect. Any integer, including 0, -100, or 9999, passes and is persisted via `Review.updateReview(reviewId, { rating })` at line 205. Contrast with the correct `||` logic at line 173 (`rating < 1 || rating > 5`) in the POST /reviews handler, confirming this is a real logic bug, not intended behavior."},
{"file":"src/routes/books.ts","line":50,"severity":"medium","issue":"Unescaped user-controlled display_name interpolated into HTML response, allowing stored XSS","evidence":"src/routes/books.ts:48-52: `res.send(`<div class=\"top-review\"><strong>${top.display_name}</strong> rated it ${top.rating}/5</div>`)`. `top.display_name` comes from `Stats.getTopReview(bookId)` (stats.ts:63-73) which joins the `users` table and returns `u.display_name` directly from the database with no HTML-escaping. Since `User.createUser`/`updateUser` in api.ts (lines 109-118, 120-129) accept arbitrary `displayName` strings with no sanitization, a user whose display_name contains `<script>...</script>` would have that script executed in any browser viewing the top-review fragment for a book they reviewed with the top rating."},
{"file":"src/routes/api.ts","line":35,"severity":"low","issue":"bookId from req.params.id is not validated as a valid number before use in /books/:id/reviews, unlike /books/:id and /books/:id/histogram which check existence","evidence":"src/routes/api.ts:34-38: `const bookId = parseInt(req.params.id as string, 10); ... res.json(Stats.getReviewsSorted(bookId, sort));` — no NaN check and no call to Book.getBookById. A request like GET /api/books/abc/reviews yields `bookId = NaN`, and `Stats.getReviewsSorted` runs `WHERE r.book_id = ?` with NaN bound, producing an empty array and 200 response, whereas `/books/:id` (line 48-57) and `/books/:id/histogram` (line 40-46) call `Book.getBookById(bookId)` and return 404 when the book doesn't exist/id is invalid. This is an inconsistency but a low-severity/minor correctness issue (not a security vulnerability)."}
],
"rejected":[]}
```

